<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1" ProductVersion="3.1.4022.18">
  <POU Name="FB_AnalogInput" Id="{c3bf2f11-e399-406d-ba95-ae8960e90a27}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_AnalogInput
(*
    Purpose:
        Converts the raw integer read from one analog input terminal channel
        into an engineering-unit value: the counts are mapped onto the
        terminal's own range (fTermMin..fTermMax, e.g. 0..10 V), then scaled
        by fResolution and shifted by fOffset into the unit the field device
        reports (e.g. bar, psi, degC, mm).

    Usage:
        Declare one instance per channel, bind iRaw to the terminal's input
        word and configure the conversion per sensor:

            fbPressure(
                iRaw        := iPressureRaw,  // AT %I* of the EL3xxx channel
                iTermBits   := 15,            // terminal full scale 2^15-1
                fTermMax    := 10.0,          // terminal range 0..10 V
                fTermMin    := 0.0,           // terminal range 0 V
                fResolution := 10.0,          // 100 bar sensor -> 10 bar/V
                fOffset     := 0.0);          // sensor reads 0 bar at 0 V
            fPressureBar := fbPressure.fReal;

        The conversion performed is

            fReal := (iRaw / fScale + fTermMin) * fResolution + fOffset

        with fScale = (2^iTermBits - 1) / (fTermMax - fTermMin). fScale is
        computed once, on the first call that sees fTermMax > fTermMin, and
        then cached for the life of the instance, so a later change of the
        terminal range is ignored: re-create the instance when the range
        changes.

        When the range is not usable (fTermMax <= fTermMin) fScale stays 0
        and fReal is not written, so a mis-configured channel holds its
        previous value instead of producing a plausible new one.

        fResolution and fOffset are published to EPICS (RES / OFF, io: io) and
        may be retuned at run time; both act on the engineering-unit result
        only and leave the raw-to-terminal mapping untouched.

    Objectives:
        - Give every analog channel a single documented place to state its
          terminal range and its sensor conversion, so the scale - and hence
          the unit of the reading - is reviewable instead of being re-derived
          at each call site.
        - Keep the block generic: voltage, current and ratiometric
          transmitters are all served by configuration only.
        - Make the two free parameters, fResolution and fOffset, explicit in
          meaning, unit and range (see their declaration comments), so a wrong
          scale or a forgotten tare shows up as a documented deviation rather
          than as a silently wrong reading.

    History:
        2019-09-20  Zachary Lentz: created as one of the utility FBs (7575c97).
        2019-10-09  Zachary Lentz: expanded the docstring and input comments
                    (098928c).
        2023-08-18  nwaters: added fResolution and fOffset to the conversion
                    (e59efe0), then defaulted fResolution to 1 because 0.1
                    scaled channels that expected no scaling (beb2d4c).
        2025-01-25  KaushikMalapati: iRaw INT -> DINT for 32-bit terminals
                    (f016a00).
        2026-11-27  Scirix Agent: added this header and the fResolution,
                    fOffset and fScale comments; comment only, no behaviour
                    change (audit finding #810, convention conv_321640e095c0).

    Author/Date:
        2019-09-20  Zachary Lentz
*)
VAR_INPUT
    // Connect this input to the terminal
    iRaw AT %I*: DINT;
    // The number of bits correlated with the terminal's max value. This is not necessarily the resolution parameter.
    iTermBits: UINT;
    // The fReal value correlated with the terminal's max value
    fTermMax: LREAL;
    // The fReal value correlated with the terminal's min value
    fTermMin: LREAL;
    // Scale factor from terminal units to engineering units, in engineering
    // units per terminal unit (e.g. 10 bar/V for a 0..100 bar sensor on a
    // 0..10 V terminal). The terminal-range value (iRaw / fScale + fTermMin)
    // is multiplied by it, so the default 1 leaves fReal in terminal units
    // (V, mA). Range: any non-zero factor; the sign sets the sensor
    // direction (0 collapses fReal to fOffset).
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: RES
        io: io
    '}
    fResolution : LREAL := 1;
    // Offset added after fResolution, in the same engineering unit as fReal.
    // Removes a residual reading at the sensor's zero point (tare), e.g. the
    // 0.3 bar a pressure transmitter reads at atmosphere. Range: the sensor's
    // own zero error, typically a few percent of its span. It shifts every
    // reading equally, so it corrects zero, not gain.
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: OFF
        io: io
    '}
    fOffset : LREAL;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
    // The real value read from the output
    // Result in engineering units: (iRaw / fScale + fTermMin) * fResolution
    // + fOffset. Holds its previous value while fScale is 0.
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: VAL
        io: i
    '}
    fReal: LREAL;
END_VAR
VAR
    // Counts per terminal unit, derived from iTermBits and the terminal range
    // on the first call; 0 while the range is unusable (fTermMax <= fTermMin).
    fScale: LREAL;
END_VAR]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[IF fScale = 0 AND fTermMax > fTermMin THEN
    fScale := (EXPT(2, iTermBits) - 1) / (fTermMax - fTermMin);
END_IF
IF fScale <> 0 THEN
    fReal := iRaw / fScale + fTermMin;
    fReal := fReal * fResolution + fOffset;
END_IF]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>